We have developed a fast, probabilistic method for approximating the permanent of any M x M real matrix, to within a small error margin. This method treats the permanent of the matrix as the expected value of the product of normally distributed random variables whose relationships we term the 'Gaussian analog' of the matrix. Our approach estimates the expected value of this product by calculating the average of the product's sampled values, repeated over a set of random selections, totaling N instances. This method takes O(MN) computational steps to produce the estimated value, achieving the accuracy bound:  \begin{equation*}
  P(|S_{N}-\text{perm}(A)| > t) \leq \frac{3^{M}}{t^{2}N}\alpha^{2M}
\end{equation*} for the quantity of matrix entries $\alpha \geq A\,$'s norm,  in cases of repeated sampling error less than a predefined, small threshold t.
